omputer interaction and visualization cite12†Networking cite13†Quantum AI cite14†Responsible AI cite15†Anti abuse cite16†Software engineering cite17†Software systems L52: \nL53: Learn More\nL54: \nL55: cite18†Publications cite19†Projects L56: \nL57: Resources\nL58: ## Building a collaborative ecosystem\nL59: \nL60: cite20†Datasets Access high-quality datasets to accelerate your research. cite21†Tools & services Explore our latest AI models and products. cite22†Open source Discover open-source code and collaborate with the community. L61: \nL62: cite30†Conferences & events L63: \nL64: Careers\nL65: ## Shaping the future together\nL66: \nL67: cite23†See all programs L68: \nL69: cite24†Faculty programs Participating in the academic research community through meaningful engagement with university faculty. cite25†Student programs Supporting the next generation of researchers through a wide range of programming. cite26†R&D programs Collaborating with industry and academic experts to support foundational science and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L70: \nL71: cite31†Blog L72: \nL73: About\nL74: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL75: \nL76: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L77: \nL78: cite32†Google Research L79: \nL80: cite33†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite34†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI†deepmind.google cite35†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google L81: \nL82: [Button: Research ]\nL83: \nL84: [Button: Resources ]\nL85: \nL86: cite30†Conferences & events L87: [Button: Careers ]\nL88: \nL89: cite31†Blog L90: \nL91: [Button: About ]\nL92: \nL93: [Input: Search]\nL94: \nL95: Search\nL96: \nL97: cite36†Image: Real-time speech-to-speech translation†storage.googleapis.com L98: # Real-time speech-to-speech translation\nL99: \nL100: November 19, 2025\nL101: \nL102: Karolis Misiunas, Research Engineer, Google DeepMind, and Artsiom Ablavatski, Software Engineer, Google Core ML\nL103: \nL104: We introduce an innovative end-to-end speech-to-speech translation (S2ST) model that enables real-time translation in the original speaker's voice with only a 2-second delay — bringing long-imagined technology into reality and making cross-language communication more natural.\nL105: Real-time communication is an integral part of both our professional and personal lives. When speaking to people remotely across language barriers, it can be difficult to truly connect by just relying on state-of-the-art translated captions, as they lack personality and real-time responsiveness essential for fluid conversation. The arrival of cite37†speech-to-speech translation (S2ST) bridges this gap by directly generating translated audio, leading to more natural communication.\nL106: Existing speech-to-speech translation systems often incur significant delays (4–5s), tend to accumulate errors, and typically lack personalization.\nL107: Today we describe an innovative end-to-end S2ST model that overcomes these limitations, enabling live translation in the original speaker's voice with only 2 second delay. The novel architecture leverages a streaming framework and, with training on time-synchronized data, significantly reduces the delay between the original input and the translated speech.\nL108: To support a breadth of languages, we introduce a scalable time-synced data acquisition pipeline that allows us to gradually expand the system to include more languages. This technology has demonstrated its effectiveness through successful deployment in real-time sensitive use cases.\nL109: ## Cascaded S2ST\nL110: \nL111: Prior real-time speech-to-speech technologies employed a cascaded pipeline of individual processing blocks:\nL112: \nL113:   1. Firstly, the source audio is transcribed to text using cite38†automatic speech recognition†en.wikipedia.org (ASR) AI models.\nL114:   2. Next, the transcribed text is translated word-for-word to the target language using cite39†automatic speech translation†en.wikipedia.org (AST).\nL115:   3. Finally, the translated text is converted back to audio using text-to-speech pipelines (TTS).\nL116: cite40†Image: RTS2ST1_Cascade†storage.googleapis.com L117: \nL118: Schematic representation of classic, cascade-style speech-to-speech translation system.\nL119: \nL120: Despite the high quality of the individual cascade components, achieving a seamless, real-time S2ST experience has been challenging due to three primary factors:\nL121:   1. Significant delays of 4–5 seconds, forcing turn-based conversations.\nL122:   2. Accumulated errors at each stage of the translation process.\nL123:   3. A notable lack of personalization due to the general-purpose TTS technology.\nL124: ## A novel end-to-end, personalized S2ST\nL125: \nL126: To significantly advance S2ST, we created a scalable data acquisition pipeline and developed an end-to-end model that provides direct, real-time language translation with just a two-second delay:\nL127:   1. Scalable data acquisition pipeline: We created a data processing pipeline to convert raw audio into a time-synchronized input/target dataset. This was achieved by integrating existing ASR and TTS technologies with precise alignment steps, ensuring translated audio best matches the input. Rigorous filtering and validation were employed to remove difficult-to-align examples.\nL128:   2. Real-time speech-to-speech translation architecture: We introduced an audio-specific streaming machine learning architecture to support training on this time-synchronized data. Building on the cite41†AudioLM framework and fundamental transformer blocks, this architecture is designed to handle continuous audio streams, allowing the model to decide when to output translations. It is also structured to manage hierarchical audio representations using the cite42†SpectroStream†arxiv.org codec technology.\nL129: An example of our personalized S2ST applied to a Spanish original translated to English.\nL130: ## Scalable data acquisition pipeline\nL131: For a given language pair, initial work starts with raw audio acquisition. We utilize a diverse set of audio sources, including data generated by TTS models. This audio undergoes a cleaning and filtering process to ensure it contains a single speaker of the source language and has an appropriate noise level. After initial data collection, an ASR step transcribes the source text.\nL132: With both source audio and text available, cite43†the forced alignment algorithm†www.danielpovey.com generates alignment timesteps (audio-to-text mapping). Any audio segments where alignment fails are discarded.\nL133: The remaining clips are machine translated from the source into the target language. Subsequently, a series of automated filters validate the translated output, ensuring accuracy and correspondence to the input text. Next, the original transcribed and translated texts are also aligned to generate corresponding timestamp annotations (text-to-translated text mapping).\nL134: Using cite41†a custom text-to-speech generation engine , the translated text is converted into translated audio, preserving the voice characteristics from the original audio while producing natural-sounding output. The pipeline concludes with one more forced alignment step of the translated text and the generated speech (speech-to-text mapping).\nL135: \nL136: cite44†Image: RTS2ST2_Pipeline†storage.googleapis.com L137: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSAM 3: Segment Anything with Concepts | Research - AI at Meta (https://ai.meta.com/research/publications/sam-3-segment-anything-with-concepts/)\nciteturn2244view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 